WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.270
- [Instructor] We are asked
what fraction is located

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.670
at point A on the number line?

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.990
And we can see point A right there.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.823
Pause this video and see
if could answer that.

00:00:11.823 --> 00:00:14.320
All right, now there's a bunch of ways

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.630
that you could think about it.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:20.390
You could see that the
space between zero and one

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.390
is split into one, two,
three, four equal spaces.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.570
And this has gone three
of those four equal spaces

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.320
from zero to one.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.950
So that's one interesting
way to think about it.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.360
Another thing that might help us

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.230
is a bit of a visualization.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.120
If this rectangle represents a whole

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.210
and notice it goes from zero to one,

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.150
so you could view one as a whole,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.020
we have split it into four equal sections.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.020
So each of these equal sections
you would consider a fourth.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.620
So that's a fourth right over there.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.400
That's another fourth right over there.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.300
This is another fourth right over there.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.360
So how many of these
fourths have been shaded in?

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.580
Well three of them have been shaded in.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.890
And when you look at the number line,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.950
you see the same idea.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.410
When we see the space between zero and one

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.290
it has been split into fourths.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.910
So this is a fourth,
and then another fourth,

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.810
and then another fourth,
and another fourth.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.320
And where is point A?

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.293
Well we have gone 1/4, 2/4, 3/4 past zero

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.890
or from zero to one, which is a whole.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.750
So what fraction is located
at point A on the number line?

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.000
3/4.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.053
Let's do another example.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:46.020
So here we're told which point
is at 2/6 on the number line.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.660
Pause this video and see if
you can answer on your own

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:51.820
before we work through it together.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.498
And I'll give you a little bit of a hint.

00:01:53.498 --> 00:01:58.498
Let's imagine that this
rectangle represents a whole

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.700
and notice it is divided
into six equal sections

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.460
so each of those sections is 1/6.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.420
And so if I start at zero,
how many would I fill in

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.830
to get 2/6?

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.830
And what would be the corresponding point

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.091
on the number line?

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:15.480
All right, now let's do it together.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.960
So if each of these is a sixth,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.530
and we have 6/6 there
so that would be a whole

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.530
and that's good because
it goes from zero to one

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.490
and you can view one as a whole.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.890
2/6 is, so that's 1/6 right over there

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.600
and then that is 2/6.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.565
And so you can see on the number line,

00:02:34.565 --> 00:02:39.565
the thing that gets us 2/6 of
the way to one is at point B.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.380
It corresponds to how much
we've filled up that rectangle,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.080
point B right over there.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.270
Now another way that you
could think about it,

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:51.890
you could see that the
space between zero and one

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.180
is split up into six equal sections.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.180
One, two, three, four,
five, six equal sections.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.700
And we want to go to 2/6.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.870
2/6.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.990
So each of those equal sections,

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.200
we are increasing by a sixth.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.180
So we're going from zero to 1/6 to 2/6.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.913
Once again we end up at point B.

